20
00:01:28,400 --> 00:01:30,700
OK. I'll look at that again.

21
00:01:30,700 --> 00:01:35,468
Then we have to describe our method of investigation in detail.

22
00:01:35,468 --> 00:01:39,370
So let's begin with how we measured wave speed.

23
00:01:39,370 --> 00:01:42,340
I was surprised how straightforward that was

24
00:01:42,640 --> 00:01:45,451
I'd expected us to have some sort of high-tech device,

25
00:01:45,451 --> 00:01:49,743
not just stand there and count the number of waves per minute.

26
00:01:49,743 --> 00:01:53,320
Not very precise, but I suppose it was good enough.

27
00:01:53,320 --> 00:01:56,860
But the way we measured the amount of salt was interesting

28
00:01:57,340 --> 00:01:58,850
In the water from the rock pools?

29
00:01:58,850 --> 00:02:02,740
Yeah, oh, I wanted to check the chemicals we used in the lab

30
00:02:02,740 --> 00:02:06,876
when we analysed those samples was it potassium chromate

31
00:02:06,876 --> 00:02:08,700
and silver nitrate? That's right.

32
00:02:08,700 --> 00:02:14,120
OK. And we need the map of the seashore. You just left that to me.

33
00:02:14,120 --> 00:02:17,445
And I had to do it while the tide was low,

34
00:02:17,445 --> 00:02:22,282
well that was OK, but the place I started it from was down on the beach,

35
00:02:22,282 --> 00:02:26,420
then I realised I should have gone up higher to get better visibility

36
00:02:26,420 --> 00:02:28,630
so I had to start all over again.

37
00:02:28,630 --> 00:02:34,720
But at least I'd got the squared paper or I'd have had problems drawing it all to scale. Yes.

38
00:02:34,720 --> 00:02:38,920
It looks good. We could get a map of the region off the internet

39
00:02:38,920 --> 00:02:41,000
and see if we need to make any changes.

40
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:46,690
I had a look but I couldn't find anything. But you took some pictures, didn't you?

41
00:02:46,690 --> 00:02:49,400
Yeah. I'll email you them if you want.

42
00:02:49,400 --> 00:02:56,250
OK. I'll make my amendments using those, then I can scan it into our report. Great.

43
00:02:56,250 --> 00:03:01,740
Now when we get to our findings

44
00:03:02,160 --> 00:03:06,187
I thought we could divide them up into the different zones we identified on the shore

45
00:03:06,187 --> 00:03:09,900
and the problems organisms face in each zone

46
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:13,100
So for the highest area... the splash zone?

47
00:03:13,100 --> 00:03:19,420
Yeah, we found mostly those tiny shellfish that have strong hard shells that act as protection.

48
00:03:19,420 --> 00:03:23,000
But not from other organisms that might eat them, predators?

49
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:25,343
No, that's not the main danger for them.

50
00:03:25,343 --> 00:03:28,560
But the shells prevent them from drying out

51
00:03:28,560 --> 00:03:31,200
because they're in the open air for most of the time. Right.

52
00:03:31,200 --> 00:03:35,820
And since they're exposed, they need to be able to find some sort of shelter,

53
00:03:35,820 --> 00:03:38,900
or cover themselves up, so they don't get too hot.

54
00:03:38,900 --> 00:03:42,680
Then in the middle and lower zones, nearer the sea

55
00:03:43,040 --> 00:03:45,500
we need to discuss the effects of wave action...

56
00:03:45,500 --> 00:03:50,540
Yes, and how organisms develop structures to prevent themselves from being swept away,

57
00:03:50,540 --> 00:03:54,030
or even destroyed by being smashed against the rocks.

58
00:03:54,030 --> 00:03:57,280
I haven't done anything on the geological changes

59
00:03:57,340 --> 00:03:58,760
I don't know what to put for that.

60
00:03:58,760 --> 00:04:05,050
No, we weren't concentrating on that. Maybe we need to find some websites.

61
00:04:05,050 --> 00:04:11,500
Good idea. I've got the lecture notes from Mr Blake's geology course, but they're too general.

62
00:04:11,500 --> 00:04:16,250
But we could ask him which books on our Reading List might be most helpful.

63
00:04:16,250 --> 00:04:22,960
Right. OK, now I did a draft of the section of sources of possible error in our research,

64
00:04:22,960 --> 00:04:25,570
